## Environment Variables

Environment variables are a common way to configure applications without changing code.

## os.Getenv & os.Setenv

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"os"
)

func main() {
	// Read an environment variable
	port := os.Getenv("PORT")
	if port == "" {
		port = "8080" // default value
	}
	fmt.Println("Listening on port:", port)

	// Set an environment variable (only for this process)
	os.Setenv("APP_ENV", "production")

	// Unset
	os.Unsetenv("DEBUG")

	// Check if a variable exists
	dbURL, exists := os.LookupEnv("DATABASE_URL")
	if !exists {
		fmt.Println("DATABASE_URL is not set")
	} else {
		fmt.Println("DB URL:", dbURL)
	}
}
```

## Reading All Environment Variables

```go
// os.Environ() returns a slice of "KEY=VALUE" strings
for _, env := range os.Environ() {
	fmt.Println(env)
}
```

## os.Args — Command-Line Arguments

`os.Args` is a string slice holding the command-line arguments:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"os"
	"strconv"
)

func main() {
	fmt.Println("Program:", os.Args[0]) // program name
	fmt.Println("Args:", os.Args[1:])   // arguments

	if len(os.Args) < 2 {
		fmt.Println("Usage: program <name> [times]")
		os.Exit(1)
	}

	name := os.Args[1]
	times := 1

	if len(os.Args) >= 3 {
		var err error
		times, err = strconv.Atoi(os.Args[2])
		if err != nil {
			fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "Error: times must be a number")
			os.Exit(1)
		}
	}

	for i := 0; i < times; i++ {
		fmt.Printf("Hello, %s!\n", name)
	}
}
```

Run with: `go run main.go Alice 3`

## The flag Package for Better CLIs

```go
package main

import (
	"flag"
	"fmt"
)

func main() {
	name := flag.String("name", "World", "Name to greet")
	times := flag.Int("times", 1, "How many times")
	flag.Parse()

	for i := 0; i < *times; i++ {
		fmt.Printf("Hello, %s!\n", *name)
	}
}
// go run main.go -name=Alice -times=3
```

